  "study_type": "INTERVENTIONAL",
  "summary": "The goal of this study is to understand why some adults continue to test positive for chlamydia or gonorrhea after receiving antibiotic treatment.\n\nThe study will also explore whether sexual health knowledge, attitudes, behaviors, and practices are linked to positive test results after treatment.\n\nThe main questions this study aims to answer are:\n\nHow many participants test positive for chlamydia or gonorrhea four or eight weeks after treatment? Are differences in sexual health knowledge, attitudes, behaviors, and practices linked to positive test results? Are treatment adherence and sexual behaviors after treatment linked to positive test results? The study will include 238 adults diagnosed with chlamydia, gonorrhea, or both infections at the Centre Hospitalier de Martigues in France.\n\nParticipants will:\n\nReceive standard antibiotic treatment as part of their usual medical care. Answer a questionnaire about their sexual health knowledge, attitudes, behaviors, and practices.\n\nAttend two follow-up visits, approximately four and eight weeks after their initial visit.\n\nProvide samples for additional laboratory tests to check for chlamydia or gonorrhea at both follow-up visits.\n\nAnswer questions about their treatment and sexual behaviors during follow-up. The researchers will compare participants who test positive after treatment with those who test negative. This comparison may help identify factors linked to persistent infection or a new infection.\n\nParticipation is voluntary. The study does not involve experimental medication or changes to the usual antibiotic treatment.",
  "title": "Factors Associated With Positive Post-Treatment STI Tests and Sexual Knowledge, Attitudes, Behaviors and Practices"
}

